===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|mid}}, {{m|enm|midde}}, from {{inh|en|ang|midd||midst, middle|pos=noun}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*midją}}, {{m|gem-pro|*midjǭ}}, {{m|gem-pro|*midjô||middle, center}} < {{m|gem-pro|*midjaz}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*médʰyos||between, in the middle, middle}}. Cognate with {{cog|de|Mitte||center, middle, midst}}, {{cog|da|midje||middle}}, {{cog|is|midja||middle}}. See also {{m|en|median}}, {{cog|la|mediānus}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} [[middle|Middle]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3|V|iii|passage=About the '''mid''' of night come to my tent.}}

===Etymology 3===
{{clipping|en|mid-range}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|disc golf}} A [[mid-range]].

===Etymology 4===
From or representing {{der|en|de|mit}}, and/or perhaps {{der|en|nds-de|mid}}. Although Middle English had a native preposition {{m|enm|mid}} with this same meaning ("with"), it had fallen out of general use by the mid 1400s<ref>{{R:New English Dictionary}}</ref> and survived into the modern English period only in sporadic use and the compounds {{m|en|midwife}} and {{m|en|theremid}}.

